# ${PROJECT_NAME} — MCP Server Catalog

> Stamped on ${TIMESTAMP}. Curated list of MCP (Model Context Protocol) servers that are likely useful for this project based on common stack signals. **None are enabled by default** — `.mcp.json` (project root) ships with `mcpServers: {}`. Enable the ones you actually use.

## How to enable a server

1. Copy the snippet from this catalog into `.mcp.json` (project root) under `mcpServers`.
2. Remove the `_doc` placeholder field if it's still there.
3. Set any required env vars (each entry below lists them).
4. Restart Claude Code (or start a new session) so the MCP server is picked up.

## Catalog (by detection signal)

| Signal in your project | Suggested MCP server | Why |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Git remote on GitHub (`origin` points at `github.com`) | `github` | Read/comment on issues, PRs, repos, releases |
| Git remote on GitLab | `gitlab` (community) | Same, for GitLab projects |
| PostgreSQL dep (`pg`, `pg-promise`, Drizzle on Postgres) | `postgres` | Schema introspection, read-only queries |
| MongoDB dep (`mongoose`, `mongodb`) | filesystem-flavoured MCP or a MongoDB-specific community MCP | Inspect collections, indexes |
| Stripe dep (`stripe` SDK) | `stripe` | Read products, prices, customers (test mode) |
| Sentry SDK (`@sentry/*`) | `sentry` | Look up issue context for debugging |
| Linear references (in `docs/`, issue comments, etc.) | `linear` | Read/update issues from inside Claude |
| Jira references | `jira` | Same, for Atlassian-based teams |
| Slack workspace mentioned in runbook | `slack` | Post deploy notes, read incident channels |
| Browser automation (`playwright`, `cypress`, `puppeteer`) | `puppeteer` | Drive a headless browser from Claude |
| Heavy docs / RAG-style retrieval | `memory` | Persistent memory across sessions |
| External research needs | `brave-search` | Web search without leaving Claude |

## Example snippets

### GitHub

```json
{
  "mcpServers": {
    "github": {
      "command": "npx",
      "args": ["-y", "@modelcontextprotocol/server-github"],
      "env": {
        "GITHUB_PERSONAL_ACCESS_TOKEN": "ghp_..."
      }
    }
  }
}
```

### PostgreSQL

```json
{
  "mcpServers": {
    "postgres": {
      "command": "npx",
      "args": ["-y", "@modelcontextprotocol/server-postgres", "postgresql://localhost:5432/${PROJECT_SLUG}"],
      "env": {}
    }
  }
}
```

### Filesystem (scoped to project)

```json
{
  "mcpServers": {
    "filesystem": {
      "command": "npx",
      "args": ["-y", "@modelcontextprotocol/server-filesystem", "${HOME}/projects/${PROJECT_SLUG}"]
    }
  }
}
```

## Picking servers

- **Start narrow.** Enable one or two servers you'll actually use this week. Don't pre-enable a long list — every server adds context to every prompt.
- **Prefer read-only by default.** Most MCP servers default to read-only; opt into writes (issue updates, DB mutations) only when you actively need them.
- **Secrets stay in env vars.** Never hardcode tokens in `.mcp.json` (project root). Read them via `${VAR}` from environment.

## Updating

To regenerate it, delete `.mcp.json` and re-run `/init-project-scaffold`; the scaffolder never overwrites an existing `.mcp.json`, even with `--force`.

For finer-grained edits, just edit `.mcp.json` directly; the file is project-owned.
